# A7 6.01–02  Triangle Inequality Theorem; Converse of the Pythagorean Theorem — MA.8.GR.1.3 (MN 6.1 + 6.2,
# merged by the plan: 6.2 is 6.1's practice). Three lengths make a triangle only when the two shorter add to MORE
# than the longest (the equal case lies flat); the third side lies between the difference and the sum; and three
# lengths make a RIGHT triangle exactly when the two shorter sides' squares add to the longest side's square — the
# longest side is the one tested, wherever it is listed. Built from the benchmark and the B1G-M first (the unit
# outline), then read against Math Nation 6.1–6.2 (A7 Unit 6 Audit).
